<h4 id="harbin-hukou-and-beijing-housing-fund">Harbin Hukou and Beijing Housing Fund</h4>

<p>I’m a coder drifting in Beijing — I went straight to Beijing after graduating from university. My hukou isn’t registered in Harbin.
Based on what I learned from asking around after visiting most of the sales offices, here’s how the housing fund works:</p>

<ol>
  <li>To use Beijing’s housing fund in Harbin, you must have a Harbin hukou.</li>
  <li>To get a Harbin hukou, you must buy a house, or have a collective hukou through employer acceptance, etc.</li>
  <li>To buy a house in <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">Harbin</code> with <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">Beijing's housing fund</code>, you must have a <strong>Harbin hukou</strong> — nowhere else will accept it.</li>
</ol>

<p>Writing this, I really want to curse, but to maintain the demeanor of a Confucian scholar, I held back. I have to say, this is a <strong>chicken-or-egg</strong> problem.</p>

<p>If rural hukou holders want to become Harbin citizens because each of us rural folk is allotted a piece of land, then I’d ask the government to take back my land and change my hukou to Harbin. If that lousy plot could make me rich, why would I have left home to drift in Beijing?</p>

