<h4 id="the-loan">The Loan</h4>

<p>After going home and handling everything, the most infuriating thing happened.</p>

<p>I paid a down payment of 610k+ and took a loan of 600k. Harbin Bank is the most [bleep] bank I’ve ever seen. My bank statements already exceeded two months of the monthly payment, but they still demanded that my girlfriend’s statements meet the same standard as mine. I was grinding my teeth at Harbin Bank in disgust. In the end, I got a commercial loan from China Merchants Bank to buy the house. At this point, I want to say: if you can meet this bank’s standards, you’re not far from the middle class — you’re already well-off among the well-off.</p>

<p>Here I’ll mention a few traps:</p>

<ol>
  <li>Bank statements for the loan must be printed on site. If your statements are fake, don’t go for the loan — you’ll gain nothing and lose everything.</li>
  <li>If your repayment ability isn’t enough, you need a co-signer. They’re required to prepare the same materials as you. This basically means relying on relatives. Unfortunately, the relative in my family who could do it already had a loan.</li>
  <li>When taking out a loan, the loan officer always recommends various wealth-management products. Don’t buy them, trust me.</li>
  <li>The bank account for the loan must have a deposit covering at least three months of repayment.</li>
</ol>

<p>I got screwed by rule <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">4</code>. After paying the down payment, saying I was penniless would be an exaggeration, but I had at most <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">2,000+</code> yuan. After covering the travel costs for two people back to Beijing, we could barely afford food and lodging. And they still wanted me to deposit <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">30,000</code>? Isn’t that ridiculous? In the end, I borrowed some money from a good friend and my sister and deposited it. Sigh, what absolute nonsense.</p>

<p>I’ve gone back to pre-liberation days. What more do they want from me?</p>

<p>By this point, I think a country where even the most basic housing for ordinary people is a problem is no longer a good country.</p>

<blockquote>
