<h4 id="withdrawing-beijings-housing-fund">Withdrawing Beijing’s Housing Fund</h4>

<p>Withdrawing Beijing’s housing fund requires the following materials:</p>

<ul>
  <li>Original and photocopy of the purchase contract</li>
  <li>Original and photocopy of the loan contract</li>
  <li>Printed proof of one continuous year or more of tax payments in Beijing</li>
  <li>Original purchase invoice and printed screenshot of the tax query</li>
  <li>Payment receipts for the purchase</li>
  <li>The company’s housing fund withdrawal form</li>
  <li>Department manager’s signed approval</li>
  <li>Personal signed statement affirming the authenticity of the provided materials</li>
</ul>

<p>After all this is done, the housing fund can be withdrawn on a scheduled basis. Since Baidu, that lousy company, has poor credit, I could only withdraw once per quarter (every 3 months).</p>

<p><strong>After completing the process, the Beijing Housing Fund will return a <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">housing fund scheduled withdrawal record form</code> to us, used as proof for others to withdraw. The next withdrawal must rely on this record form — for example, if my girlfriend wants to withdraw, she must have this record form. Remember, this is very important.</strong></p>

<p>The first payment didn’t arrive until the end of June. The first thing I did was pay back all the money I’d borrowed for the house.</p>

<blockquote>
  <p>I just can’t figure out why my own housing fund money is so hard to withdraw. Even with all the meetings and paperwork reductions, I found that non-Beijingers are still stuck in the same old rut — only Beijingers can withdraw with ease. With a chest full of anger, I started despising that taxi driver again.</p>
</blockquote>

<p>After such complicated procedures, I finally managed to buy an apartment. Thinking about it is truly bitter and helpless — sad and dispirited. Buying a house in China is just this troublesome. To those who’ll buy houses later: prepare for a protracted war, because we’re wrestling wits and guts with the Chinese system.</p>

<p>If you have any questions about my house-buying experience, please leave a comment at the bottom — I’ll answer them one by one.</p>

<p>That’s the end of the house-buying story. I believe my future neighbors are no ordinary people — they must have gone through at least part of what I did: income, status, all of it. Because those who can afford a place in the Aida plot are either rich or influential. I hope my experience and house-buying tips can serve as a reference so you can avoid the detours.</p>

